----
Hibernate flush-listener fork for Play! framework (Hibernate ORM 7.4.x)
----

The Play! "willBeSaved" semantics (flush-time cascades and collection actions
must ignore JPABase entities that are not going to be saved) live in the
framework's own forked flush event listeners:

    framework/src/play/db/jpa/PlayAbstractFlushingEventListener.java
        fork of org.hibernate.event.internal.AbstractFlushingEventListener

    framework/src/play/db/jpa/PlayFlushEventListener.java
        fork of org.hibernate.event.internal.DefaultFlushEventListener

    framework/src/play/db/jpa/PlayAutoFlushEventListener.java
        fork of org.hibernate.event.internal.DefaultAutoFlushEventListener

    framework/src/play/db/jpa/PlayHibernateIntegrator.java
        registers the two listeners (EventType.FLUSH / EventType.AUTO_FLUSH),
        wired from JPAPlugin via JpaSettings.INTEGRATOR_PROVIDER

All Play-specific changes inside the fork files are wrapped in
"// [play] ... // [/play]" marker comments.

This patch (hibernate-7.4.14-patch-play-fork.patch) is a record of exactly
those changes. It is NOT applied to hibernate-core itself: it re-applies the
Play changes on top of a freshly copied upstream listener, i.e. it is used to
re-create the forks after upgrading Hibernate ORM.

----
How to re-create the forks after upgrading Hibernate ORM
----

1. Download the Hibernate ORM source of the target version.

2. Copy the three upstream files (verbatim) into framework/src/play/db/jpa/
   applying only the mechanical renames:

       package org.hibernate.event.internal -> package play.db.jpa
       AbstractFlushingEventListener        -> PlayAbstractFlushingEventListener
       DefaultFlushEventListener            -> PlayFlushEventListener
       DefaultAutoFlushEventListener        -> PlayAutoFlushEventListener

   Keep the upstream license headers, javadoc and comments as they are.

3. Verify the copies compile as they are (this is the pristine fork state,
   functionally identical to vanilla Hibernate):

       cd framework && ant compile

4. Apply the Play changes:

       DRY RUN -> patch --dry-run -p1 -i patches/hibernate-<version>-patch-play-fork.patch
       APPLY   -> patch -p1 -i patches/hibernate-<version>-patch-play-fork.patch
       (run from the framework/ directory)

   Note: upstream refactorings between Hibernate versions may shift the patched
   code; fix up the marker blocks manually and regenerate the patch file from
   the diff between the pristine copies and the final forks.
